<div class="tribe-manual">
  <ManualHeader />

  <ManualPageNav @activePage="auth" />

  <div class="container pb-5">

    <div class="steps-container mb-5">

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Overview access levels none read full Junction frontend server"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">1</span>
          <span class="step-title">Overview</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Tribe decides an access level for every request to
            <code>api.php</code>,
            <code>search.php</code>
            and
            <code>uploads.php</code>. There are three levels.</p>
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Level</th>
                  <th>How a request gets it</th>
                  <th>What it allows</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>None</strong></td>
                  <td>No key, an unknown key, or a key used from a domain that is
                    not whitelisted.</td>
                  <td>Read public objects. Submit live public forms.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Read</strong></td>
                  <td>A read-only API key.</td>
                  <td>Read public and private objects.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Full</strong></td>
                  <td>A full-access API key, or a Junction session.</td>
                  <td>Read, create, update and delete. Run
                    <code>?sql=</code>
                    queries. Upload files.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mb-0">Each kind of client reaches these levels in its own
            way:</p>
          <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Junction</strong>
              logs in with a password and gets a session cookie.</li>
            <li><strong>Frontend apps</strong>
              send a read-only API key.</li>
            <li><strong>Server-side code</strong>
              sends a full-access API key from its own
              <code>.env</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Junction login session cookie auth.php backend-for-frontend password"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">2</span>
          <span class="step-title">Junction Login (Session)</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Junction uses a backend-for-frontend login. The browser holds only
            an HttpOnly session cookie. Tribe holds the API key and uses it on
            the browser's behalf.</p>

          <h6 class="mt-3"><strong>Login flow</strong></h6>
          <ol class="small">
            <li>Junction sends the password to
              <code>auth.php?action=login</code>.</li>
            <li>Tribe compares it with
              <code>JUNCTION_PASSWORD</code>
              on the server.</li>
            <li>On a match, Tribe starts a
              <code>tribe_session</code>
              cookie and stores a full-access key in the session.</li>
            <li>Every later Junction request carries the cookie, and Tribe
              gives it full access.</li>
          </ol>

          <h6 class="mt-3"><strong>Endpoints</strong></h6>
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Request</th>
                  <th>Body</th>
                  <th>Response</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>GET /auth.php?action=status</code></td>
                  <td>None</td>
                  <td><code>{ "authenticated": true }</code>
                    or
                    <code>false</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>POST /auth.php?action=login</code></td>
                  <td><code>{ "password": "..." }</code></td>
                  <td>200 on success, 401 on a wrong password, 429 after too
                    many failures</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>POST /auth.php?action=logout</code></td>
                  <td>None</td>
                  <td>200, and the session is destroyed</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h6 class="mt-3"><strong>Session rules</strong></h6>
          <ul class="small">
            <li>The cookie is HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax. It is also Secure when
              Tribe is served over HTTPS.</li>
            <li>A session expires after 12 hours without use, or 7 days after
              login.</li>
            <li>After 10 wrong passwords from one IP address within 15 minutes,
              login is refused for the rest of that window.</li>
          </ul>

          <h6 class="mt-3"><strong>Trusted origins</strong></h6>
          <p class="small">Tribe accepts the session cookie, and answers
            <code>auth.php</code>, only for requests from these origins:</p>
          <ul class="small">
            <li>The origin of
              <code>JUNCTION_URL</code>.</li>
            <li><code>http://localhost:JUNCTION_PORT</code>
              and
              <code>http://127.0.0.1:JUNCTION_PORT</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small">This origin check is what protects the session from
            cross-site request forgery.</p>

          <div class="alert alert-info mb-0">
            <strong>Same site required:</strong>
            Junction and Tribe must be on the same site, such as two subdomains
            of one domain, or two ports on
            <code>localhost</code>. Browsers block the session cookie between
            unrelated domains.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Junction session key apikey_record managed_by revoke logout password change"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">3</span>
          <span class="step-title">The Junction Session Key</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>On the first login, Tribe creates an
            <code>apikey_record</code>
            titled
            <strong>Junction session</strong>, with
            <code>managed_by</code>
            set to
            <code>junction_session</code>. Every later login reuses this
            record. Its key never leaves the server.</p>
          <p class="mb-1">You can end Junction sessions in three ways:</p>
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small mb-3">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Action</th>
                  <th>Effect</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Change
                    <code>JUNCTION_PASSWORD</code></td>
                  <td>Every session ends. Users log in with the new
                    password.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Delete the Junction session record, or make it
                    read-only</td>
                  <td>Every session ends. The next login creates a new
                    key.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Log out from the 3 dots menu</td>
                  <td>Only that browser's session ends.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-warning mb-0">
            <strong>Do not reuse this key.</strong>
            The Junction session key has full access. Create a separate key for
            every other app.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Frontend apps API key bearer read-only whitelisted domains devmode Junction API"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">4</span>
          <span class="step-title">Frontend Apps (API Keys)</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>A frontend app that uses Tribe as its backend sends an API key as
            a Bearer token:</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-3">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('Authorization: Bearer your-read-only-key', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>Authorization: Bearer your-read-only-key</code>
          </div>

          <h6><strong>Create a key</strong></h6>
          <ol class="small">
            <li>Log in to Junction, open the 3 dots menu on the top right
              corner, and choose
              <strong>Junction API</strong>.</li>
            <li>Create a new key and set
              <strong>Read-only</strong>
              to on.</li>
            <li>Enter the app's domains in
              <strong>Whitelisted domains</strong>, one per line.</li>
            <li>Set the privacy to
              <code>public</code>
              or
              <code>private</code>, then save and copy the key.</li>
          </ol>

          <h6 class="mt-3"><strong>Key settings</strong></h6>
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Setting</th>
                  <th>Effect</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Read-only</strong></td>
                  <td>On gives read access. Off gives full access.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Whitelisted domains</strong></td>
                  <td>The key works only when the request's
                    <code>Origin</code>
                    host, or its
                    <code>Referer</code>
                    host when there is no Origin, matches a line.
                    <code>*.example.com</code>
                    matches every subdomain. An empty list means the key works
                    from anywhere.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Dev mode</strong></td>
                  <td>The key also works from
                    <code>localhost</code>
                    and
                    <code>127.0.0.1</code>, even when they are not
                    whitelisted.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Privacy</strong></td>
                  <td>Only
                    <code>public</code>
                    and
                    <code>private</code>
                    keys work. A key in any other state, such as
                    <code>draft</code>, is ignored.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="alert alert-warning">
            <strong>Use only read-only keys in the browser.</strong>
            Anyone can read a key that ships in frontend code, and anyone can
            fake the
            <code>Origin</code>
            header outside a browser. A whitelist stops other websites from
            using the key, but it does not keep the key secret.
          </div>

          <p class="mb-1">Private objects are returned only when the request
            asks for them:</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-3">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('GET /api.php/post?show_public_objects_only=false', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>GET /api.php/post?show_public_objects_only=false</code>
          </div>

          <div class="alert alert-info mb-0">
            <strong>Ember setup:</strong>
            See the
            <LinkTo @route="frontend">Frontend</LinkTo>
            page for how to put the key in an ember-tribe app.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Server-side apps full-access key dist-php PHP scripts env"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">5</span>
          <span class="step-title">Server-side Apps (Full Access)</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Code that runs on a server can hold a full-access key, because
            visitors never see it. This includes PHP in
            <code>dist-php</code>, scheduled jobs and other backends.</p>
          <ol class="small">
            <li>Create a key in
              <strong>Junction API</strong>
              with
              <strong>Read-only</strong>
              off.</li>
            <li>Leave
              <strong>Whitelisted domains</strong>
              empty. Server-to-server requests usually send no
              <code>Origin</code>
              header, so a whitelisted key would be refused.</li>
            <li>Store the key in that server's
              <code>.env</code>, and send it as a Bearer token.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="command-block mb-0">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('curl -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $TRIBE_API_KEY&quot; https://tribe.domain.tld/api.php/post', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TRIBE_API_KEY"
              https://tribe.domain.tld/api.php/post</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Anonymous access public objects public forms block_read_access_without_apikey webapp"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">6</span>
          <span class="step-title">Anonymous Access &amp; Public Forms</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>A request without a valid key has no access level, and Tribe
            applies these rules:</p>
          <ul class="small">
            <li>Lists and single objects return only objects with
              <code>content_privacy</code>
              set to
              <code>public</code>.</li>
            <li><code>GET /api.php/webapp</code>
              returns a reduced blueprint, with only the
              <code>webapp</code>
              entry and the types whose public form is live. Object counts are
              left out.</li>
            <li>A POST is accepted only for a type whose public form is live.
              Tribe saves it as
              <code>private</code>
              and removes reserved fields such as
              <code>id</code>,
              <code>user_id</code>
              and
              <code>role_slug</code>.</li>
            <li>Anonymous POSTs to
              <code>user</code>,
              <code>apikey_record</code>
              and
              <code>blueprint_record</code>
              are always refused.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="alert alert-info mb-0">
            <strong>Private installs:</strong>
            Set
            <code>block_read_access_without_apikey</code>
            to
            <code>true</code>
            in the blueprint to refuse every anonymous read except the reduced
            <code>webapp</code>
            blueprint.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="SQL queries uploads search endpoint rules full access"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">7</span>
          <span class="step-title">SQL, Uploads &amp; Search</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6><strong>SQL queries</strong></h6>
                  <p class="small">The
                    <code>?sql=</code>
                    parameter needs full access. Tribe accepts only one
                    <code>SELECT</code>
                    statement, with no comments, no
                    <code>INTO</code>, no locking clauses and no sleep
                    functions.</p>
                  <p class="small mb-0">The query runs inside a read-only
                    transaction, which is always rolled back.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6><strong>Uploads</strong></h6>
                  <p class="small mb-0"><code>uploads.php</code>
                    needs full access to upload files and to use the Dist
                    Uploads actions. Files already uploaded are still served
                    publicly from
                    <code>/uploads/</code>.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6><strong>Search</strong></h6>
                  <p class="small mb-0"><code>search.php</code>
                    returns only public objects by default. It returns private
                    objects only when
                    <code>TYPESENSE_SHOW_PUBLIC_OBJECTS_ONLY=false</code>
                    and the request has a valid key.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Environment variables env JUNCTION_PASSWORD JUNCTION_URL JUNCTION_PORT ALLOW_ALL_CONNECTIONS_DANGEROUSLY CORS"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">8</span>
          <span class="step-title">Environment Settings</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>These settings in Tribe's
            <code>.env</code>
            control authentication:</p>
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Variable</th>
                  <th>Purpose</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>JUNCTION_PASSWORD</code></td>
                  <td>The Junction login password. Login is refused while it
                    is empty.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>JUNCTION_URL</code></td>
                  <td>The exact address Junction is served from, including
                    scheme and port. Its origin is trusted for sessions.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>JUNCTION_PORT</code></td>
                  <td>Trusts
                    <code>localhost</code>
                    and
                    <code>127.0.0.1</code>
                    on this port for sessions.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>ALLOW_ALL_CONNECTIONS_DANGEROUSLY</code></td>
                  <td>When
                    <code>true</code>, every request gets full access. Keep it
                    <code>false</code>
                    on any server that others can reach.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small mb-0">Browsers on trusted origins get credentialed
            CORS responses. Every other origin gets
            <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>
            without credentials, so Bearer keys work from anywhere but
            cookies do not.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Troubleshooting 403 401 429 forbidden login fails errors"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge"><i
              class="fa-solid fa-screwdriver-wrench"
            ></i></span>
          <span class="step-title">Troubleshooting</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm small mb-0">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Symptom</th>
                  <th>Likely cause</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Junction login returns 403</td>
                  <td><code>JUNCTION_URL</code>
                    does not match the address Junction is served from.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Login succeeds, then Junction returns to the login
                    page</td>
                  <td>Junction and Tribe are on different sites, so the
                    browser drops the cookie.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Login returns 429</td>
                  <td>Too many wrong passwords. Wait 15 minutes.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A frontend app gets 403 or only public objects</td>
                  <td>The domain is not whitelisted, or the key is not
                    <code>public</code>
                    or
                    <code>private</code>, or the request did not send
                    <code>show_public_objects_only=false</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A write returns 403</td>
                  <td>The key is read-only.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>?sql=</code>
                    returns 400</td>
                  <td>The query is not a single read-only
                    <code>SELECT</code>.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <ManualFooter />

  </div>
</div>

<ManualScripts />
